 By Mike Allen &middot; Nov 26, 2026 
 
 
 🦃 Gobble, gobble! Happiest Thanksgiving! We're grateful to you for making Axios AM part of your morning, and for helping make this the world's most interesting and consequential breakfast table. 

 🧤 I'm filing from Scott and Sheri's home in Raleigh. Lots of you know my nephew Evan, our lax player, from White House and Axios events over the past 20+ years. Now young Evan is a Dad: 2-year-old Jack is pioneering the next generation of Allens. I hope you meet sneaky tiger Jack at an Axios shindig soon. Smart Brevity™ count: 1,691 words ... 6½ mins. Thanks to Noah Bressner for the holiday orchestration. And to Bill Kole for the feast-morning magic. 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Grateful for growth 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Lindsey Bailey/Axios. Photo: Library of Congress

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Each Thanksgiving, Axios Macro's Neil Irwin and Courtenay Brown find five reasons to be thankful about the economy. And there are lots: 

 1️⃣ The economy is ripping. Wall Street has been reeling amid fears about the AI boom's future. But right now, the U.S. economy is growing fast.

 It grew at a 4.3% annual rate from July through September, according to revised GDP data, well above the 3.4% first reported. 2️⃣ The productivity revival. After years of lackluster productivity growth, the U.S. economy is getting better at producing more with the same amount of labor.

 That gives businesses room to raise wages without increasing prices, and lets the economy grow faster without fueling inflation. Labor productivity has grown at a 2.5% annual pace since early 2023, compared with 1.5% from 2005 to 2022, according to San Francisco Fed research. 3️⃣ The entrepreneurship boom. Americans filed more than 5 million applications to start businesses in the first 10 months of the year — the fastest pace on record, according to Census Bureau data.

 Early signs suggest AI is making it easier to start and run a business without hiring staff. The boom is also evident in data from payments company Stripe. The number of new businesses launching on its platform each week has roughly doubled from a year ago, CEO Patrick Collison said last week — a rate of growth without precedent in Stripe's 15-year history. 4️⃣ Low, low layoffs. A year ago, there was deep angst about the labor market as companies looked to an AI-powered future and the unemployment rate edged up.

 The unemployment rate has held steady at low levels this year, and layoffs are near all-time lows. The rate at which employers laid off or discharged workers was 1% in September, a tick above the 0.9% low in data that dates to 2001. 5️⃣ America's financial dominance. The U.S. remains at the center of the global financial system, giving American companies an extraordinary ability to raise money to finance growth and innovation.

