 2. 🩺 Pros and cons of Medicare for All 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Aïda Amer/Axios. Stock: Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Medicare for All is getting a big boost in attention after the midterms. Here's a primer for your Thanksgiving table arguments, via Axios' Adriel Bettelheim and David Nather: 

 The boldest idea in health care comes down to one massive tradeoff: Guaranteed coverage for everyone in exchange for an eye-popping price tag and an ultra-messy transition.

 Why it matters: With huge majorities in both chambers, Democrats can try to force a vote when they're in power, although they're likely to find that not everyone in the party is on board. Sen.-elect Abdul El-Sayed (D-Mich.) — a major supporter of Medicare for All who wrote a book about it — is driving the conversation with an offer to name the plan "TrumpCare."

 Trump reposted his offer and said he'd meet with El-Sayed, telling the New York Post : "Maybe there's some place in the middle that we could go." ✅ The pros: Medicare for All would replace the nation's fragmented insurance system with universal coverage that's subject to a single standard.

 In theory, that would eliminate the gaps that left 28 million Americans uninsured last year, according to the most recent CDC figures. The coverage would also be "portable," meaning you'd keep the same arrangement if you change jobs or move to a different state. For life. The basic benefits would also be more generous than most existing public and private insurance plans. ⚠️ The cons: Sticker shock. The projected 10-year cost of implementing a plan is about $47 trillion, per the White House Council of Economic Advisers, which predicts higher taxes, more deficit spending and program cuts.

 There's also the transition to a new system. Sen. Bernie Sanders' 2017 bill would have ended most existing forms of insurance, including workplace coverage, within four years. How the government would handle continuity of medical care during that time is a big unknown. (El-Sayed supports allowing "additional private insurance through unions or employers" so it's not all wiped out.) Critics predict delays in getting medical tests and treatments. 🥊 Reality check: El-Sayed and Trump will probably meet, and it could end with mutual praise and hopeful vibes. El-Sayed likes to flatter Trump. Trump likes to be flattered.

 But don't take that as a sign that a deal is imminent. There's pretty much zero common ground, other than calling it TrumpCare. Trump hates Medicare for All. Share this story ... Get Axios Future of Health Care . 

 
 
