 4. 🌧️ Macy&#x27;s parade bummer 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Workers inflate balloons in preparation for the now-postponed Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade yesterday in Manhattan. Photo: Yuki Iwamura/AP For the first time in its 100-year history, the Macy's Thanksgiving Day Parade has been postponed because of weather, Axios' Josephine Walker and Julianna Bragg write. 

 An impending nor'easter forced organizers to push the parade to Saturday. 🥣 Morning chefs, rejoice! Your traditional food-prep soundtrack awaits: NBC will broadcast last year's parade today at 8:30 a.m. in all time zones. The postponed parade will air live at 8:30 a.m. Saturday.

 A grounded balloon depicts Goku from Dragon Ball Z. Photo: Bing Guan/Reuters 🎈 The big picture: Macy's was expected to celebrate its centennial parade with a record 100 inflatables.

 Huge balloons can't fly when sustained winds exceed 23 mph or gusts exceed 34 mph. Last evening near Central Park, famous balloons — the Pillsbury Doughboy, Snoopy, the Wimpy Kid — looked melancholy, "half-inflated, as if in existential limbo," the N.Y. Times notes . 

 Parade officials have only grounded the giant character balloons once before, in 1971, because of weather. 📺 Between the lines: The parade is one of the few non-sports events that still draws tens of millions of Americans to watch the same thing at the same time.

 Last year, it drew a record 35.7 million viewers across NBC and Peacock. Share this story . 

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 5. ⛽ Road trip relief 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Data: AAA. Chart: Noah Bressner/Axios U.S. gas prices have dropped about 70¢ a gallon over the past month — giving Thanksgiving drivers a break at the pump today, Axios' Ben Geman writes. 

 AAA says regular gas averages $3.56 today. 🛢️ What's happening: More oil is making its way out of the Middle East, bringing relief to the global market.

 U.S. demand is also down from the summer, and refiners have switched to cheaper winter blends. Between the lines: Gas is still 18% more expensive than a year ago, but the recent drop could boost holiday travel and consumer spending, Raymond James analyst Pavel Molchanov said.

